module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyImpossible }
import gunbc.rung_climb { AwaitsOneGrounding, GroundingUndeclared }

data a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_killed_serve_process_holds_its_ledger_charge_until_the_lease_lapses" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: StructurallyImpossible,
      blocker: AwaitsOneGrounding { grounding: GroundingUndeclared },
      trigger_capability: "the remote-build serving settle path writes the attempt's ending into the host's capacity ledger when the attempt's process is gone -- observed by the serving entry on its own exit AND by the host's own supervisor on the unit's death -- so a charge's lifetime is owned by something that cannot outlive the work it charges; sufficient for a killed holder to leave nothing to lapse"
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE: a compute reservation is charged to a host's capacity ledger under an ATTEMPT REFERENCE (gunbc.compute.host_capacity: the reservation records `reference` and an `expires_at`), and the only thing that ends the charge is the fold that reads the ledger (PoolLapsed inside the reservation fold, host_capacity.dag:667) or an explicit settle. Nothing in the serving path notices that the process the charge was made for is gone. A process killed by signal -- not ended, not settled -- therefore leaves its charge standing, and the ledger has no field that distinguishes a charge whose holder is running from a charge whose holder is a corpse.**",

    "FOUND: 2026-11-20, srv1, on the remote-build serving entry. At about 01:20Z a ctrl worker's `pgrep -x claim_batch` sweep killed srv1 PIDs 1821238 and 1821317 with SIGKILL; one was a cargo build under the remote-build serve, so its serve process died while holding a build-pool reservation. The client of that run would have been answered by the transport, not by the work -- an abnormal end, exactly the case that leaves the ledger untouched.",

    "HARM, AND ITS SIZE IS THE LEASE. The charge keeps its share of the pool appropriated until it lapses, so a host whose build pool is one CI slot can read CAPACITY-FULL -- and answer every later request with HostCapacityFull, which the gate client exits as 3 'no host admitted' -- for as long as `compute_reservation_term_seconds` (host_capacity.dag:293, 7200s) allows. On a one-slot pool that is the whole required gate, for two hours, for a commit that has done nothing wrong. The state is silent to the requester, which sees a legitimate capacity fact, and silent to the fleet: no reader of the ledger distinguishes live from dead, and gunbc.fleet.host_capacity_publish has no consumer at all, so the pool's occupied share is not something the fleet can even be asked about.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS, THE ONES THAT SEPARATE THIS FROM AN ORDINARY LEAK: the charge is a row in a table keyed by an attempt reference, while the LIMIT it stands for is a kernel object (the unit's memory.max) that dies with its holder -- so the enforcement is already self-clearing and only the ACCOUNTING outlives the work; a lapse is computed at read time by comparing `expires_at` to a clock, never written back, so the ledger's own record of the past two hours stays 'occupied'; and the settle that would release it early exists as a typed ending (WorkCancelled in the attempt's ending path, work_provider_local.dag:1058 -- 'a settle the provider itself could not complete is infrastructure and says nothing about the code'), reached only when a provider process runs it.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- bounded by the lease, which is why this is a row and not an outage: the maximum damage of any one killed holder is one lease term, and the term is a declared constant rather than a wait nobody can bound. CURRENT RUNG: mitigatable, by the same lease. CEILING: structurally impossible -- once a charge's lifetime is carried by the same object that carries the bound it reserves (the attempt's cgroup, or a settle written by whoever supervises that cgroup's death), a holder that dies cannot leave a charge behind; there is no moment at which the accounting and the kernel disagree. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, stated so it cannot be satisfied by a narrower repair: THE SERVING PATH SETTLES THE ATTEMPT WHEN ITS HOLDER DIES -- the serve writing its own ending on exit AND the host's supervisor settling a unit that died by signal -- at which point the lease stops being the mitigation and becomes only a backstop for a host that was unreachable while the work ran. Naming 'check the lease is short enough' as the trigger would satisfy itself while the class stayed alive everywhere else, because a bound on the outage is not a rule that ends it.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, ONE QUESTION: for every resource this corpus charges to a table, name the object that ENFORCES that charge, and ask what happens to the table entry when the enforcing object disappears on its own. If the answer is 'nothing notices', the row is in this class, and the size of the harm is whatever timeout the table carries.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
